{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness to determine preference. Skewness refers to the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. A lottery is left-skewed if a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (higher payoff) compared to unfavorable ones (zero payoff). In both lotteries, the favorable outcome is a positive amount (6000 or 3000 Euros), and the unfavorable outcome is 0 Euros. The probability mass is entirely concentrated on the two outcomes: a positive payoff with a small probability and zero otherwise. To assess skewness, we compare the probability assigned to the favorable outcome. Lottery B has a 0.1% chance of 6000 Euros, while Lottery A has a 0.2% chance of 3000 Euros. Since 0.2% > 0.1%, Lottery A has a higher probability mass on the favorable outcome (3000 Euros) compared to Lottery B (6000 Euros). Thus, Lottery A is more left-skewed. The decision reason prefers the more left-skewed lottery, which would be Lottery A. Therefore, the decision reason is applicable and can be applied to this decision problem.",
  "step_2": "The individual states: 'Die Wahrscheinlichkeiten sind bei beiden Optionen gering. Daher habe ich die Option mit mehr Geld gewählt.' This translates to: 'The probabilities in both options are low. Therefore, I chose the option with more money.' The individual acknowledges that both lotteries have low probabilities and concludes that they selected the one with a higher monetary payoff (6000 Euros vs. 3000 Euros). The individual does not mention probability distribution, skewness, or any analysis of how probability is distributed across outcomes. Instead, the justification is based solely on the size of the monetary outcome — choosing the one with more money, regardless of probability. Thus, the considered information is limited to the payoff amounts and the observation that probabilities are low. No mention is made of skewness or probability distribution patterns.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The individual focuses only on the magnitude of the monetary payoff, not on the probability distribution or the relative concentration of probability on favorable outcomes. The decision reason requires evaluating which lottery has a greater probability mass on the favorable outcome — i.e., a more left-skewed distribution. In this case, Lottery A has a higher probability (0.2% vs. 0.1%) on a smaller payoff (3000 Euros), making it more left-skewed than Lottery B. However, the individual does not reference this distributional property. Instead, the justification is based on the size of the payoff, not on skewness. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The justification does not align with the rule of preferring the more left-skewed lottery. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

